# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
*.egg-info/

# Subagent-driven-development scratch (ledger, task briefs/reports, review packages).
# Local-only working state; may contain absolute paths, so never track it.
.superpowers/

# Local config overlays hold secrets and personal data. Never commit these.
# (sluice.yaml.example is the tracked template; a real sluice.yaml / *.local.yaml
# holds your personal searches, contact details, and hosts.)
*.local.yaml
sluice.local.yaml
/sluice.yaml
*.token
google_token*.json
.env
.env.*

# Runtime artifacts (CV render/serve/apply outputs, dossiers, dedup db,
# health/disabled tracking files)
cv-out/
cv-output/
cv-served/
cv-home/
dossiers/
*.pdf
*_health.json
*_disabled.json
*.db

# AI-tool configs are GENERATED from .rulesync/ via `rulesync generate`. The source of
# truth is .rulesync/ and it IS tracked; everything below is a build artifact. Run
#   npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run rulesync
# after cloning to populate them locally. Byte-identical to what CI runs, `--ignore-scripts`
# included: a human following a command CI does not run executes install scripts CI
# deliberately skips, and tests/test_ci_wiring.py now fails the build if the two drift.
#
# Every pattern is ROOT-ANCHORED, and the leading slash is load-bearing: a bare
# `CLAUDE.md` matches at ANY depth and silently swallows the canonical source at
# .rulesync/rules/CLAUDE.md, leaving git with no rules file to clone.
#
# ONLY WHAT THE CURRENT `-t` CAN WRITE lives in this block. Five entries that used to sit here
# were emitted by targets the narrowing dropped; they moved to LEGACY OUTPUTS below, so this
# header's claim ("everything below is populated by npm run rulesync") is true of its own block
# again rather than of five-eighths of it.
/CLAUDE.md
/AGENTS.md
/.claude/
# Not written today, but REACHABLE, which is why it stays here rather than moving down: `-f '*'`
# includes the `mcp` feature and `claudecode` supports it, so this file appears the moment
# someone adds `.rulesync/mcp.json`.
#
# AND THIS ENTRY IS THE ONLY THING THAT WOULD CATCH IT. An earlier note here claimed the drift
# guard reds on the new feature term; re-measured on the pinned version, that is false in the
# case that matters. With a source holding an EMPTY `mcpServers` map -- what a placeholder or a
# half-finished commit looks like -- the run writes `/.mcp.json` and prints a summary
# BYTE-IDENTICAL to the green one: `12 file(s) total (2 rules + 5 subagents + 4 skills + 1
# hooks)`, no `mcp` term, the total unmoved despite a 13th file on disk. guard_rulesync_drift.py
# exits 0. It reds only once that source actually names a server, and then on `unparsed text`
# rather than on the feature, because rulesync prints `1 MCP files` and the guard's term pattern
# is lower-case. So there is no second gate behind this line -- which is a reason to keep it,
# not a reason to trust something else. Its reason for being absent (no source file) is one file
# away from being undone; a legacy entry's reason (no target) is not.
/.mcp.json

# LEGACY OUTPUTS -- no longer generated. THE IGNORE ENTRIES are what cannot be removed; the
# files themselves can be, by hand, on each machine that has them. (The earlier wording read as
# though the artifacts were undeletable, which is the opposite of the problem.)
#
# package.json's rulesync script targets `claudecode,agentsmd`; under the previous `-t '*'` it
# emitted an output for every tool rulesync knew about, and every machine that ran that command
# still has these paths on disk. Narrowing `-t` stops them being REGENERATED; it cannot delete
# what already exists. Drop these ignores and a single `git add -A` commits ~46k lines of build
# output -- measured, on this branch, by doing exactly that.
#
# So this list is now STATIC rather than growing: a new upstream target never needs adding here,
# because it is never generated. Re-audit only when `-t` is WIDENED, or when a new rulesync
# FEATURE is turned on (`-f '*'` covers rules/ignore/mcp/subagents/commands/skills/hooks/
# permissions). scripts/guard_rulesync_drift.py pins the per-feature output counts and fails CI
# until a human looks here.
#
# NOTE: `.github/` is TRACKED (workflows/), so only rulesync's generated SUBdirs and files under
# it are ignored here -- never a bare `.github/`.
/.agents/
/.aiassistant/
/.augment/
/.cline/
/.codex/
/.cursor/
/.cursorrules
/.deepagents/
/.devin/
/.factory/
/.github/agents/
/.github/copilot-instructions.md
/.github/hooks/
/.github/skills/
/.goose/
/.goosehints
/.grok/
/.hermes/
/.hermes.md
/.junie/
/.kilo/
/.kiro/
/.opencode/
/.pi/
/.qwen/
/.reasonix/
/.roo/
/.roomodes
/.rovodev/
/.rules
/.takt/
/.vibe/
/.warp/
/GEMINI.md
/QWEN.md
/REASONIX.md
/replit.md

# Belt and braces: re-include the canonical source unconditionally. The `/**` is required --
# a bare `!/.rulesync/` re-includes only the DIRECTORY and cannot rescue a file inside it.
!/.rulesync/**

# npm toolchain for the rulesync generator (CI only; sluice itself is stdlib-only Python).
# Root-anchored like the generated-output block above.
/node_modules/
# NOT root-anchored, deliberately -- same reasoning as .memsearch/ below. Root-anchoring exists
# only to stop a bare `CLAUDE.md` swallowing the canonical source at .rulesync/rules/CLAUDE.md,
# a collision a credential file cannot cause. .npmrc can carry a registry auth token, and it is
# also how an `npm_config_*` setting could switch on a dependency's install-time script, so
# catching it at any depth is the safer default.
.npmrc

# MemSearch runtime artefacts: session transcripts carrying absolute home paths, session
# UUIDs and PIDs. Never in a public repo.
#
# Deliberately NOT root-anchored, unlike everything above. The anchoring above exists to
# stop a bare `CLAUDE.md` swallowing the canonical source at .rulesync/rules/CLAUDE.md --
# a collision that cannot happen here, since nothing legitimate is named .memsearch/. For
# a directory that has already leaked personal data into this repo twice, catching it at
# ANY depth is the safer default, and there is nothing for it to shadow.
.memsearch/
